      "text": "To find the inverse of $\\mathbf{A}^2$, we first note that $\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}$, where $\\mathbf{I}$ is the identity matrix. Therefore,\n$\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}$.\nTaking the inverse on both sides, we have\n$\\left(\\mathbf{A} \\mathbf{A}^2\\right)^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}^{-1} = \\mathbf{I}$.\nExpanding the left-hand side, we get\n$\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{I}$.\nApplying $\\mathbf{A}^{-1}$ on both sides, we have\n$\\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{A}^2 = \\mathbf{A}^{-1} \\mathbf{I} = \\mathbf{A}^{-1}$.\nTherefore, the inverse of $\\mathbf{A}^2$ is $\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}}$.The answer is: \\begin{pmatrix}2&5\\ -1&-3\\end{pmatrix}",
